Question.2) Overloading vs Overriding

class Parent {
    void display(Number n) {
        System.out.println("Parent Number");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display(Number n) {
        System.out.println("Child Number");
    }

    void display(Integer n) {
        System.out.println("Child Integer");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Parent p = new Child();
        p.display(10);
    }
}


================================Solution========================================
Parent p = new Child();

This creates

Reference -----> Parent
Object --------> Child

##Compiler checks the method
p.display(10);
Compiler asks: "What methods does Parent have?"
Parent only has display(Number)
It does NOT know about display(Integer) because p is a Parent reference.
So compiler chooses display(Number)

Notice something important.

The compiler never even considers    display(Integer)

##Runtime starts
Now JVM knows the actual object is
new Child()
So it asks
"Is display(Number) overridden?"
Yes.

class Child {

    void display(Number n) {
        System.out.println("Child Number");
    }

}

Therefore JVM executes
Child Number

Output              Child Number

##Why NOT Child Integer?

People think

10

↓

Integer

↓

display(Integer)

But that never happens.
Because overload resolution has already finished.
Compiler already selected

display(Number)

Runtime cannot change the parameter list.

Runtime only decides

Which overridden version of the same signature should run?

Not

Which overloaded method should be called?








